# The lambda calculus, the foundation of functional computation, modeled and RUN by the kernel. Where the SKI combinators
# (computation/combinator.tree) compute with three variable-free constants, the lambda calculus computes with BOUND
# VARIABLES and substitution. A term is one of three shapes: a variable, a lambda abstraction (an anonymous function of
# one argument), or an application of one term to another. The single computation rule is BETA REDUCTION: applying a
# lambda to an argument substitutes that argument into the lambda's body. These two systems are the two halves of the
# same theory. Every lambda term translates to an SKI combinator term and back, so they compute exactly the same
# functions, and both are Turing-complete. The lambda calculus is the named-variable face, SKI the variable-free face.
#
# DE BRUIJN INDICES. Naming bound variables invites capture: substituting a term that mentions `x` into a lambda that
# also binds `x` silently changes its meaning. De Bruijn indices remove names entirely. A variable is a natural number
# counting how many lambdas lie between its use and the lambda that binds it. Index 0 is the nearest enclosing lambda,
# index 1 the one outside that, and so on. The identity function `lambda x . x` becomes `lambda (variable 0)`. The
# constant function `lambda x . lambda y . x` becomes `lambda (lambda (variable 1))`: inside two lambdas, the outer
# binder is one level out, so it is index 1. With indices there are no names to capture, so substitution is purely
# mechanical: it only has to renumber the free indices as terms cross lambda boundaries.
#
# BETA REDUCTION. `apply (lambda body) arg` reduces to `substitute body arg`: the argument is dropped in for index 0
# throughout the body, every other free index is decremented by one (its binder is gone), and indices inside the argument
# are shifted up as it descends under lambdas. The `shift` and `substitute` tasks below are that machinery, written total
# (every index case handled). The reducer is FUEL-BOUNDED: `reduce` applies a leftmost-outermost head `step` a fixed
# number of times, so it always halts and the kernel computes normal forms. This is the same fuel discipline as
# combinator.tree's `reduce`, and it lets every law below check by the kernel reducing both sides to one normal form.

# A natural number: zero, or the successor of a natural. Used for de Bruijn indices AND for reduction fuel.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# A lambda term in de Bruijn form: a variable (an index counting enclosing lambdas), a lambda (an anonymous one-argument
# function wrapping a body term), or an application of a function term to an argument term.
form term
  case variable
    link index, like natural
  case lambda
    link body, like term
  case apply
    link function, like term
    link argument, like term

# a two-case truth value, defined locally as a matchable form so the kernel unfolds it under `fork case` (the native
# boolean does not reduce through the proof checker). yes means the test holds, no means it does not.
form flag
  case yes
  case no

# a three-way comparison result: the left is below, equal to, or above the right.
form ordering
  case below
  case equal
  case above

# compare two naturals three ways, by recursion on both. zero vs zero is equal, zero vs succ is below, succ vs zero is
# above, and succ vs succ strips one level off each. Total and terminating (descends on both arguments together).
task compare
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like ordering
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make equal
        case succ
          send back
            make below
    case succ
      link a-prior
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make above
        case succ
          link b-prior
          send back
            call compare
              read a-prior
              read b-prior

# is the index `i` at least the cutoff `c`? (c <= i). yes when comparing i to c gives equal or above, no when below.
# Decides whether an index is FREE relative to a cutoff, the test `shift` needs at each variable.
task at-least
  take i, like natural
  take c, like natural
  like flag
  send back
    call at-or-above
      call compare
        read i
        read c

# is an ordering equal-or-above? (the "at least" test, separated so its scrutinee is a plain matched value).
task at-or-above
  take order, like ordering
  like flag
  fork case, read order
    case below
      send back
        make no
    case equal
      send back
        make yes
    case above
      send back
        make yes

# shift every FREE index in a term up by one, where "free" means at-or-above the cutoff `c`. Bound indices (those below
# the cutoff, introduced by lambdas inside the term) are left alone. Descending under a lambda raises the cutoff by one.
# This is the renumbering that keeps an argument's free variables pointing at the right binders as it moves under lambdas
# during substitution. Total: every variable either shifts or stays, and the structural cases recurse on smaller terms.
task shift
  take c, like natural
  take t, like term
  like term
  fork case, read t
    case variable
      send back
        call shift-variable
          call at-least
            read index
            read c
          read index
    case lambda
      send back
        make lambda
          bind body
            call shift
              make succ
                bind prior
                  read c
              read body
    case apply
      link shift-function
      link shift-argument
      send back
        make apply
          bind function
            call shift
              read c
              read shift-function
          bind argument
            call shift
              read c
              read shift-argument

# rebuild a variable after the shift test: yes increments the index (it is free, so it shifts up), no leaves it (it is
# bound below the cutoff). Split out so the dispatch matches a plain flag, which the kernel unfolds transparently.
task shift-variable
  take move, like flag
  take index, like natural
  like term
  fork case, read move
    case yes
      send back
        make variable
          bind index
            make succ
              bind prior
                read index
    case no
      send back
        make variable
          bind index
            read index

# substitute the term `value` for the de Bruijn index `depth` throughout `t`. At a variable: if it equals `depth` the
# value is dropped in (shifted up `depth` times on the way down, here `value` is pre-shifted by the caller at depth 0 and
# re-shifted under each lambda); if it is a free index above `depth` it is decremented by one (the binder it counted past
# is being removed); otherwise it is left unchanged. Descending under a lambda raises `depth` by one and shifts `value`
# up by one so its free variables keep pointing outward. Beta reduction calls this at depth zero. Total over every index.
task substitute
  take depth, like natural
  take value, like term
  take t, like term
  like term
  fork case, read t
    case variable
      send back
        call substitute-variable
          read depth
          read value
          read index
    case lambda
      send back
        make lambda
          bind body
            call substitute
              make succ
                bind prior
                  read depth
              call shift
                make zero
                read value
              read body
    case apply
      send back
        make apply
          bind function
            call substitute
              read depth
              read value
              read function
          bind argument
            call substitute
              read depth
              read value
              read argument

# the predecessor of a natural (the index minus one), defaulting zero's predecessor to zero. Used to decrement a free
# index whose binder is being removed by the substitution.
task predecessor
  take n, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      send back
        read prior

# substitute at a single variable index, comparing it against the substitution depth three ways. EQUAL means the index
# is the one being substituted, so the value is dropped in unchanged. ABOVE means a free index past the removed binder,
# so it is decremented by one. BELOW means an index bound inside the body, left unchanged. This is the clean, total
# de Bruijn variable rule, with no renumbering of the value itself (the lambda case already shifts the value as it
# descends).
task substitute-variable
  take depth, like natural
  take value, like term
  take index, like natural
  like term
  send back
    call dispatch-substitution
      call compare
        read index
        read depth
      read value
      read index

# dispatch on the three-way comparison of the index against the substitution depth. EQUAL means the index is the one
# being substituted, so the value is dropped in unchanged. ABOVE means a free index past the removed binder, so it is
# decremented. BELOW means an index bound inside the body, left unchanged. Split out from substitute-variable so the
# scrutinee is a plain matched value, which the kernel unfolds transparently.
task dispatch-substitution
  take order, like ordering
  take value, like term
  take index, like natural
  like term
  fork case, read order
    case equal
      send back
        read value
    case above
      send back
        make variable
          bind index
            call predecessor
              read index
    case below
      send back
        make variable
          bind index
            read index

# ONE leftmost-outermost head beta reduction step. The only redex is an application whose function is a lambda:
# `apply (lambda body) arg` fires to `substitute 0 arg body`. If the function is not yet a lambda, the step reduces the
# function part (walking down the left spine toward the head), keeping the argument in place. A bare variable or a lambda
# has no head redex and is returned unchanged, so the step is total.
task step
  take t, like term
  like term
  fork case, read t
    case variable
      send back
        read t
    case lambda
      send back
        read t
    case apply
      link head-function
      link head-argument
      fork case, read head-function
        case lambda
          # the beta redex: substitute the argument for index 0 in the lambda's body.
          link lambda-body
          send back
            call substitute
              make zero
              read head-argument
              read lambda-body
        case variable
          send back
            read t
        case apply
          # head redex lies deeper: reduce the function part, keep the argument.
          send back
            make apply
              bind function
                call step
                  read head-function
              bind argument
                read head-argument

# the fuel-bounded normalizer: apply `step` exactly `n` times. The fuel strictly decreases, so `reduce` always halts and
# the kernel computes it on any concrete term, checking the equations below by reflexivity. Mirrors combinator.tree.
task reduce
  take n, like natural
  take t, like term
  like term
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        read t
    case succ
      link fuel-prior
      send back
        call reduce
          read fuel-prior
          call step
            read t

# UNIVERSAL: zero fuel reduces nothing. reduce zero t = t, for every term t. The base of the normalizer, definitional.
rule reduce-zero-is-identity
  seat t, like term
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reduce
        make zero
        read t
      read t
  calm hold

# THE IDENTITY APPLIED. `(lambda (variable 0)) x` beta-reduces to `x` in one step, for a concrete argument x (here the
# identity itself). This is `lambda x . x` run on an argument: substitute the argument for index 0 in the body `variable
# 0`, which is exactly the argument. The defining computation of the lambda calculus.
rule identity-applied-returns-its-argument
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step
        make apply
          bind function
            make lambda
              bind body
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
          bind argument
            make lambda
              bind body
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
      make lambda
        bind body
          make variable
            bind index
              make zero
  calm hold

# UNIVERSAL: the identity fires on ANY argument. step (apply (lambda (variable 0)) x) = x, for every term x. This is the
# law `(lambda x . x) y = y` in full generality, not just a concrete instance: the body `variable 0` substitutes to the
# argument unchanged for every x.
rule identity-beta-reduces-any-argument
  seat x, like term
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step
        make apply
          bind function
            make lambda
              bind body
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
          bind argument
            read x
      read x
  calm hold

# THE CHURCH K COMBINATOR, the constant function `lambda x . lambda y . x`, in de Bruijn form `lambda (lambda (variable
# 1))`: inside two lambdas, the outer binder is one level out, so the body is index 1. Applied to two arguments it returns
# the FIRST and discards the second, matching SKI's K (combinator.tree's k-discards-its-second-argument). Here K applied
# to (variable 0) and (variable 1) reduces in two head steps to the first argument (variable 0). The matching SKI rule is
# K a b = a, the same constant-selection behaviour reached through substitution instead of a primitive rewrite.
rule church-k-keeps-the-first-argument
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reduce
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
        make apply
          bind function
            make apply
              bind function
                make lambda
                  bind body
                    make lambda
                      bind body
                        make variable
                          bind index
                            make succ
                              bind prior
                                make zero
              bind argument
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
          bind argument
            make variable
              bind index
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
      make variable
        bind index
          make zero
  calm hold

# CHURCH BOOLEANS, the lambda-calculus selectors. true = `lambda x . lambda y . x` = `lambda (lambda (variable 1))` (it
# is literally the K combinator above) and false = `lambda x . lambda y . y` = `lambda (lambda (variable 0))`. An
# if-then-else is the boolean applied to two branches, `b then else`, picking the then-branch when true and the else when
# false. The two rules below RUN that program on concrete branches and show it selects correctly: a real lambda program.

# if true then a else b = a. With true = lambda (lambda (variable 1)), the term (true a) b reduces to a (here a =
# variable 0, b = variable 1) in two head steps. The same selection SKI does with true = K.
rule church-true-selects-the-then-branch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reduce
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
        make apply
          bind function
            make apply
              bind function
                make lambda
                  bind body
                    make lambda
                      bind body
                        make variable
                          bind index
                            make succ
                              bind prior
                                make zero
              bind argument
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
          bind argument
            make variable
              bind index
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
      make variable
        bind index
          make zero
  calm hold

# if false then a else b = b. With false = lambda (lambda (variable 0)), the term (false a) b reduces to b (here a =
# variable 0, b = variable 1) in two head steps. The same selection SKI does with false = K I.
rule church-false-selects-the-else-branch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reduce
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
        make apply
          bind function
            make apply
              bind function
                make lambda
                  bind body
                    make lambda
                      bind body
                        make variable
                          bind index
                            make zero
              bind argument
                make variable
                  bind index
                    make zero
          bind argument
            make variable
              bind index
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
      make variable
        bind index
          make succ
            bind prior
              make zero
  calm hold
